    <h1>Foundations for Inference</h1>
    <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mb-6">OpenIntro Statistics &middot; Ch. 5 &middot; <a href="https://www.openintro.org/book/os/">openintro.org/book/os</a></p>

    <div class="bg-zinc-800 rounded-lg p-5 mb-8 callout">
      <p class="text-lg" style="max-width:none"><strong>Statistical inference</strong> draws conclusions about a population from a sample. Two main tools: <strong>confidence intervals</strong> estimate a parameter with a margin of error, and <strong>hypothesis tests</strong> evaluate whether evidence supports a claim. Both rest on the sampling distribution.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="stats-book"><p>Keep the population fixed and watch how its sample means vary. <a href="/reading/statistics/estimates-vary/">Read the guided introduction →</a></p><SamplingDistribution client:visible  /></div>

    <!-- 1. Point Estimates and Sampling Distribution -->
    <h3>Point estimates and the sampling distribution</h3>
    <p>A <strong>point estimate</strong> is a single number computed from data (like the sample mean). Different samples give different estimates. The distribution of all possible estimates is the <strong>sampling distribution</strong>. By the <a href="/reading/probability/grinstead-09/">🎰 Central Limit Theorem</a>, means of independent observations from the same distribution with finite, positive variance are approximately normal for sufficiently large n. The population itself need not be normal.</p>

    <!-- SVG: Sampling distribution with rejection regions -->
    <div class="flex justify-center my-6">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 400 180" class="w-full max-w-md" style="font-family: ui-monospace, monospace;">
        <!-- Axes -->
        <line x1="30" y1="140" x2="370" y2="140" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-opacity="0.3"/>
        <!-- Bell curve (sampling distribution) -->
        <path d="M 30,140 Q 60,138 80,135 Q 100,128 120,115 Q 140,92 160,65 Q 180,35 200,25 Q 220,35 240,65 Q 260,92 280,115 Q 300,128 320,135 Q 340,138 370,140" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/>
