    <SchemeRepl client:visible initialCode={`; Point estimate: sample mean as estimate of population mean
; Standard error: SD / sqrt(n)

(define sample '(67 72 74 68 71 69 73 70 75 66))

(define (mean lst)
  (* 1.0 (/ (apply + lst) (length lst))))

(define (std lst)
  (let ((m (mean lst)))
    (sqrt (/ (apply + (map (lambda (x) (* (- x m) (- x m))) lst))
             (- (length lst) 1)))))

(define x-bar (mean sample))
(define s (std sample))
(define n (length sample))
(define se (/ s (sqrt n)))

(display "Sample mean: ") (display x-bar) (newline)
(display "Sample SD:   ") (display s) (newline)
(display "Sample size: ") (display n) (newline)
(display "Std error:   ") (display se)
`} />


    <!-- 2. Confidence Intervals -->
    <h3>Confidence intervals</h3>
    <p>A confidence interval combines a point estimate with a margin of error. For independent observations from a normal population with unknown standard deviation, a <strong>95% t interval</strong> is x̄ &plusmn; t* &times; s/√n. Here n = 10, so there are 9 degrees of freedom and t* ≈ 2.262. Under those assumptions, the procedure covers the population mean in 95% of repeated samples. See <a href="/reading/statistics/intervals/">the interval experiment</a> for the coverage idea.</p>

    <SchemeRepl client:visible initialCode={`; 95% confidence interval for the mean
; CI = x-bar +/- t* * SE
; Independent normal observations; sigma unknown. Keep n = 10 for this cutoff.

(define sample '(67 72 74 68 71 69 73 70 75 66))

(define (mean lst)
  (* 1.0 (/ (apply + lst) (length lst))))
(define (std lst)
  (let ((m (mean lst)))
    (sqrt (/ (apply + (map (lambda (x) (* (- x m) (- x m))) lst))
             (- (length lst) 1)))))

(define x-bar (mean sample))
(define se (/ (std sample) (sqrt (length sample))))
(define t-star 2.262157)  ; 95% confidence, df = 9 (n = 10)

(define margin (* t-star se))
(define ci-lower (- x-bar margin))
(define ci-upper (+ x-bar margin))

(display "Point estimate: ") (display x-bar) (newline)
(display "Margin of error: ") (display margin) (newline)
(display "95% CI: (")
(display ci-lower) (display ", ")
(display ci-upper) (display ")") (newline)
(newline)
(display "We are 95% confident the population mean") (newline)
(display "falls between ") (display ci-lower)
(display " and ") (display ci-upper)
`} />

    <div class="python-block my-2" style="display:none">
      <Repl client:visible initialCode={`# Confidence interval in Python
import math

sample = [67, 72, 74, 68, 71, 69, 73, 70, 75, 66]
n = len(sample)
x_bar = sum(sample) / n
s = math.sqrt(sum((x - x_bar)**2 for x in sample) / (n - 1))
se = s / math.sqrt(n)

t_star = 2.262157  # 95% confidence, df = 9; this cutoff assumes n = 10
margin = t_star * se

print(f"Point estimate: {x_bar:.1f}")
print(f"Margin of error: {margin:.2f}")
print(f"95% CI: ({x_bar - margin:.2f}, {x_bar + margin:.2f})")
`} />
    </div>


    <!-- 3. Hypothesis Testing -->
    <h3>Hypothesis testing and p-values</h3>
    <p>Start with a <strong>null hypothesis</strong> H&#x2080;, such as a specified population mean. The <strong>p-value</strong> is the probability, under the null model, of a test statistic at least as extreme as observed according to the chosen test. Set a decision threshold &alpha; before seeing the data. The example below uses a two-sided t test with 9 degrees of freedom and independent normal observations; its critical value assumes the ten observations shown.</p>

    <SchemeRepl client:visible initialCode={`; Hypothesis test: is the population mean = 70?
; H0: mu = 70
; HA: mu != 70 (two-tailed)

(define sample '(67 72 74 68 71 69 73 70 75 66))
(define mu-0 70)  ; null hypothesis value

(define (mean lst)
  (* 1.0 (/ (apply + lst) (length lst))))
(define (std lst)
  (let ((m (mean lst)))
    (sqrt (/ (apply + (map (lambda (x) (* (- x m) (- x m))) lst))
             (- (length lst) 1)))))

(define x-bar (mean sample))
(define se (/ (std sample) (sqrt (length sample))))

; Test statistic: t = (x-bar - mu0) / SE
(define t-stat (/ (- x-bar mu-0) se))

(display "Sample mean:    ") (display x-bar) (newline)
(display "Null value:     ") (display mu-0) (newline)
(display "Std error:      ") (display se) (newline)
(display "t-statistic:    ") (display t-stat) (newline)
(newline)

; Decision at alpha = 0.05
; Reject if |t| > 2.262157
(define alpha 0.05)
(display "Critical value: 2.262157") (newline)
(display "|t| = ") (display (abs t-stat)) (newline)
(display "Reject H0? ")
(display (if (> (abs t-stat) 2.262157) "Yes" "No"))
`} />


    <!-- 4. Significance Level -->
    <h3>Significance level and errors</h3>
    <p>The <strong>significance level</strong> &alpha; is the threshold for rejecting H&#x2080;. Type I error: rejecting H&#x2080; when it is true (probability at most &alpha; for a valid level-&alpha; test). Type II error: failing to reject H&#x2080; when it is false (probability = &beta;). <strong>Power</strong> = 1 - &beta;: the probability of correctly detecting a real effect.</p>

    <SchemeRepl client:visible initialCode={`; Error types in hypothesis testing

;                 H0 true       H0 false
; Reject H0:     Type I (a)    Correct (power)
; Fail to reject: Correct      Type II (b)

(define alpha 0.05)
(display "Significance level (alpha): ") (display alpha) (newline)
(display "Type I error is controlled at alpha = ") (display alpha) (newline)
(newline)

; If the true effect is small, power is low
; Power increases with: larger sample, larger effect, larger alpha
(define beta 0.20)  ; typical target
(define power (- 1 beta))
(display "P(Type II error) = beta = ") (display beta) (newline)
(display "Power = 1 - beta = ") (display power) (newline)
(newline)

; Trade-off: lowering alpha reduces Type I errors
; but increases Type II errors (lowers power)
(display "Lower alpha -> fewer false positives,") (newline)
(display "             -> more false negatives")
`} />


